ngs cluster at Likely or Solid R and markets price Green near 10%, so a modest shift toward Rogers from the fundamentals-only baseline is warranted.", "watch": "Green's strong fundraising and consolidated post-primary coalition could narrow the race if Rogers' residency questions gain traction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Neal Dunn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 2nd District is an open Republican-held seat after Neal Dunn's retirement, with a partisan lean of R+8. Republican Austin Rogers won his primary and enters the general with a major financial advantage, having raised over $1 million and benefiting from about $1.4 million in outside support. Democrat Amanda Green has consolidated her party after a contested primary and has raised roughly $350,000, a respectable but smaller total.\n\nExpert raters mostly place the race in the Likely or Solid Republican column, with one rating it Lean R, and prediction markets give Green roughly a one-in-ten chance. No reliable general-election polling exists, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, money, and ratings, all of which favor Rogers in this rural Panhandle district.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -8.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.084, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1162, "margin": -8.5}]}